---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
---
<Base
  title="The UK Property Buying Guide for NRIs and Overseas Buyers | Salet"
  description="The full Salet buying guide: the UK purchase process end to end for NRI and overseas buyers."
  noindex
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="The buying guide · Full text"
    title="The UK Property Buying Guide for NRIs and Overseas Buyers"
    lede="Written by Salet, the buyer-only property concierge. This is general information for buyers new to the UK process, not financial, tax or investment advice."
    compact
  />

  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16 lg:px-8 lg:py-24">
    <div class="prose-salet">
      <h2>01 · How the UK purchase process actually works</h2>
      <p>
        The English and Welsh system has a shape worth memorising: <strong>search, offer, conveyancing, exchange,
        completion</strong>. You find a property and make an offer through the seller's estate agent. If accepted,
        both sides instruct solicitors and the legal work begins: searches, enquiries, survey, mortgage valuation.
        Nothing is binding yet. Weeks later, contracts are exchanged and deposits paid; from that moment the deal is
        legally enforceable. Completion, usually one to four weeks after exchange, is when money moves and keys change
        hands.
      </p>
      <p>
        The single most important fact for buyers from India: <strong>everything before exchange is reversible by
        either side</strong>. An accepted offer is a statement of intent, not a contract. Scotland runs a different
        and earlier-binding system; this guide describes England and Wales, where most overseas purchases happen.
      </p>

      <h2>02 · Leasehold and freehold</h2>
      <p>
        Freehold means you own the building and the land indefinitely. Leasehold, which covers a large share of
        flats, means you own a long tenancy: a lease with a fixed term remaining, a service charge for the building,
        possibly a ground rent, and a freeholder whose consent matters for alterations. Lease length drives value and
        mortgageability: a lease around 80 years or below is a serious warning line, because extending becomes
        expensive and buyers and lenders retreat.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before offering on any leasehold property, know the remaining term, the service charge history, the ground
        rent terms and the state of the building's management. Ask for the last two years of service charge accounts.
        The lease is the asset; the flat is just where you stand while you own it.
      </p>

      <h2>03 · What everything costs</h2>
      <p>
        Beyond the price itself, budget for: <strong>Stamp Duty Land Tax</strong>, which rises in bands with price
        and rises again for additional properties and for non-resident buyers, so your residency status and what you
        already own change the bill materially; <strong>legal fees</strong> for your conveyancer plus search fees;
        <strong>survey costs</strong>; <strong>mortgage and broker fees</strong> where financed; and on flats, the
        recurring <strong>service charge and ground rent</strong>. Then the ownership costs: council tax, insurance,
        maintenance, letting and management fees if you rent it out, and income tax on UK rental income even for
        non-resident landlords.
      </p>
      <p>
        Rates and bands change with government budgets, so check current figures at gov.uk or with your solicitor at
        the time you buy, and take tax advice from a qualified adviser for your own position. The discipline that does
        not change: price the whole purchase, not the purchase price.
      </p>

      <h2>04 · Financing and the documents lawyers will demand</h2>
      <p>
        UK residents with UK income access the ordinary mortgage market. Buyers resident in India can finance UK
        property too, through international arms of major banks and specialist lenders, typically at lower
        loan-to-value ratios and with more documentation. A mortgage broker experienced with overseas files is worth
        their fee in approvals alone; Salet introduces clients to brokers but takes nothing for doing so.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whatever the funding route, prepare for <strong>anti-money-laundering checks</strong> that surprise most
        overseas buyers with their depth. Your solicitor is legally required to verify identity and the source of
        your funds: bank statements showing accumulation, sale documents for assets sold, gift letters where family
        contributes. Money coming from India also moves under the Reserve Bank of India's remittance rules, which cap
        annual outward remittances per person; plan the transfer route and timing with your bank early. Start
        assembling the paper trail before you start viewing, because the buyer whose file is ready moves weeks faster
        than the buyer whose file is a promise.
      </p>

      <h2>05 · Finding and reading property</h2>
      <p>
        The portals show you most of the market. Two further layers matter: <strong>sold prices</strong>, which are
        public record and tell you what streets actually trade at rather than what sellers hope; and the
        <strong>off-market layer</strong>, homes sold quietly through agents' relationships before listing. Access to
        the second comes from being a credible, ready buyer that agents want to deal with, or from representation
        that already has the relationships.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read listings as advocacy, not information. The photographs are the property's best hour. The floor plan and
        the street's sold-price history are closer to the truth, and the viewing, ideally more than one, at different
        times, is where truth lives.
      </p>

      <h2>06 · Offers, gazumping and the undefended weeks</h2>
      <p>
        Offers go through the seller's agent, in writing, ideally with proof of funds attached, because a documented
        buyer is a believable one. Once accepted, you enter the undefended weeks: the period before exchange when the
        seller can still accept a higher offer (gazumping), demand more late (gazundering has a buyer-side cousin),
        or simply withdraw. It is uncomfortable and it is legal.
      </p>
      <p>
        The defences are speed and posture: solicitor instructed the day the offer is accepted, survey booked
        immediately, mortgage application in motion, responsive communication throughout, and a relationship with the
        selling agent that makes you the buyer they advise their client to keep. Slow buyers get gazumped; prepared
        buyers exchange.
      </p>

      <h2>07 · Surveys, negotiation and completion day</h2>
      <p>
        Do not confuse the lender's <strong>valuation</strong> with a <strong>survey</strong>; the first protects the
        bank, the second protects you. On older stock, a full building survey is money well spent. Survey findings are
        also the second negotiation: documented issues justify revisiting the price or requiring works, with evidence
        rather than sentiment.
      </p>
      <p>
        At exchange you commit and pay your deposit; between exchange and completion you arrange buildings insurance
        (your risk usually starts at exchange) and final funds. Completion day is mechanical when prepared: money
        flows solicitor to solicitor, keys release at the agent, meters get read, and someone, you or your
        representative, walks the empty property before signing off.
      </p>

      <h2>08 · After the keys: holding from abroad</h2>
      <p>
        If the property will be let, the landlord obligations are real and enforced: deposit protection, gas and
        electrical safety, licensing where applicable, and the non-resident landlord tax framework for owners abroad.
        A letting agent handles the daily churn; the question overseas owners forget is who supervises the agent,
        reviews the statements and stands in the property once a year. If it will stand empty, insurance policies
        typically impose inspection conditions on unoccupied homes, and post accumulating behind a door is both a
        security signal and a compliance risk.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether through Salet's oversight and care services or arrangements of your own, decide who owns these jobs
        before completion, not after the first problem.
      </p>

      <div class="rule mt-14"></div>
      <p class="mt-10 text-sm leading-relaxed text-stone">
        This guide is general information about the buying process in England and Wales, correct in spirit at July
        2026; rules, rates and bands change. It is not financial, tax, legal or investment advice, and Salet does not
        provide those services. For your own position, take advice from qualified professionals, and check current
        government guidance at gov.uk.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTABand
    title="The guide is the map. The territory is harder."
    body="If you would rather walk it with representation, start with a 30 minute discovery call. Your brief, the process, and an honest view on fit."
  />
</Base>
